sensors: t41: gc5603: move the output crop when the image is flipped

Vertical flip left a band of optical black along one edge of the picture.

The sensor reads a window of 1674 rows and the crop takes 1620 of them
starting 4 rows in, so 54 rows are spare. Flipping reverses the readout,
so those 4 rows are consumed at the far end instead, and rows meant to
stay outside the picture come into it. The complement, 54 - 4 = 50, puts
the crop back where it was relative to the sensor.

Measured rather than assumed: 50 removes the band exactly, and the
arithmetic predicted it, so this is not a fitted constant.

Only the crop moves. The readout window start was the other candidate and
turned out not to be involved, so it is left alone - moving it would have
shifted the exposure geometry for nothing.

The same reasoning predicts 88 - 4 = 84 for horizontal mirror, whose
window is 2968 wide against a 2880 crop, but no band has been observed
there and an unverified constant is worse than none, so that is left until
somebody sees one.

--- a/kernel/sensors/t41/gc5603/gc5603.c
+++ b/kernel/sensors/t41/gc5603/gc5603.c
@@ -711,6 +711,24 @@
 	val = gc5603_read(sd, 0x022c, &val);
 	val1 = gc5603_read(sd, 0x0063, &val1);
 
+	/*
+	 * The output crop has to follow the flip, or a band of optical black
+	 * appears at the edge of the picture.
+	 *
+	 * The sensor reads a window of 1674 rows (0x034a/0x034b) and the crop
+	 * takes 1620 of them (0x0096/0x0097) starting 4 rows in (0x0099), so 54
+	 * rows are spare. Flipping reverses the readout, so those 4 rows are
+	 * consumed at the far end instead and rows meant to stay outside the
+	 * picture come into it. Taking the complement, 54 - 4 = 50, puts the crop
+	 * back where it was relative to the sensor.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on hardware: 50 removes the band exactly, first try.
+	 */
+	if (enable & 0x02)
+		gc5603_write(sd, 0x0099, 50);
+	else
+		gc5603_write(sd, 0x0099, 4);
+
 	/* 2'b01 mirror; 2'b10 flip; 2'b11 mirror &flip */
